{{Australian federal election, 2016 sidebar}}

A '''federal election''' was held on Saturday 2 July 2016 to elect all 226 members of the 45th [[Parliament of Australia]], after an extended eight-week official campaign period. It was the first [[double dissolution]] election since the [[Australian federal election, 1987|1987 election]]. On 10 July, eight days after the election took place, Opposition Leader [[Bill Shorten]] conceded defeat, acknowledging that the incumbent [[Liberal Party of Australia|Liberal]]–[[National Party of Australia|National]] [[Coalition (Australia)|Coalition]] would certainly be able to form a government, either in a [[Majority government|majority]] or [[Minority government|minority]] status.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-07-10/election-2016-result-bill-shorten-concedes-defeat/7584400|title=Election 2016: Malcolm Turnbull claims victory after Bill Shorten concedes defeat|date=10 July 2016|accessdate=10 July 2016|author=Monique Ross|publisher=Australian Broadcasting Corporation}}</ref> On 11 July, the [[Australian Broadcasting Corporation|ABC]] declared that the Coalition had won enough seats to form a majority government.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://mobile.abc.net.au/news/2016-07-11/lnp-retains-capricornia-has-76-seats/7587578|title=Election 2016: LNP retains Capricornia, gives Coalition 76-seat majority government|date=11 July 2016|accessdate=11 July 2016|publisher=Australian Broadcasting Corporation}}</ref>

In the first week of vote counting after the election, no party had won enough seats in the [[Australian House of Representatives|House of Representatives]] to form a government.<ref name="abc month">{{cite web |url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-07-03/election-2016-ballot-count-could-take-a-month-finalise,-aec-says/7565158|title=Election 2016: Ballot count could take a month to finalise, AEC says |author= |publisher=Australian Broadcasting Corporation |date=4 July 2016|accessdate=4 July 2016}}</ref><ref name="smh tuesday">{{cite web |url=http://www.smh.com.au/federal-politics/federal-election-2016/australian-federal-election-2016-no-results-until-at-least--tuesday-20160702-gpx9qz.html|title=Australian federal election 2016: No results until at least ... Tuesday|author=Deborah Gough |publisher=Sydney Morning Herald |date=3 July 2016|accessdate=4 July 2016 }}</ref><ref name="daily mail">{{cite web |url=http://www.dailymail.co.uk/news/article-3671086/Federal-election-2016-exit-poll-shows-result-close-call-3-4-cent-swing-Labor-62-cent-believe-Malcolm-Turnbull-win.html|title='I can form a majority government': Malcolm Turnbull's confident he'll win the election and avoid a hung parliament as Bill Shorten praises Labor's 'magnificent campaign'... but there may not be a result until TUESDAY|author=Jennifer Smith |publisher=Daily Mail Australia |date=3 July 2016|accessdate=4 July 2016 }}</ref> Following initial results, neither [[Malcolm Turnbull]] of the [[Coalition (Australia)|Coalition]], nor Shorten of the [[Australian Labor Party|Labor Party]], made a [[concession (politics)|concession]],<ref name="sky liberal majority">{{cite web |url=http://www.skynews.com.au/news/top-stories/2016/07/04/federal-election-outcome-still-unknown.html|title=Liberals 'cautiously optimistic' on majority|author=|publisher=Sky News Australia |date=4 July 2016|accessdate=4 July 2016 }}</ref><ref name="smh shorten">{{cite web |url=http://www.smh.com.au/federal-politics/federal-election-2016/australian-federal-election-2016-bill-shorten-says-malcolm-turnbull-should-quit-20160704-gpy3ec.html|title=Australian federal election 2016: Bill Shorten says Malcolm Turnbull 'should quit'|author=Fergus Hunter|publisher=Sydney Morning Herald|date=4 July 2016|accessdate=4 July 2016 }}</ref> while many predicted a [[hung parliament]].<ref name="daily mail" /><ref name="australian hung">{{cite web |url=http://www.theaustralian.com.au/federal-election-2016/federal-election-2016-heres-the-sequel-hung-parliament-ii/news-story/acbf4787b8b2aaf16f3e0c9e37e2ff36|title=Federal election 2016: here's the sequel, Hung Parliament II|author=Sid Maher|publisher=The Australian |date=4 July 2016|accessdate=4 July 2016 }}</ref><ref name="guardian hung">{{cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2016/jul/03/turnbull-and-shorten-court-independents-with-hung-parliament-in-play|title=Turnbull and Shorten court independents with hung parliament in play|author=Lenore Taylor|publisher=The Guardian Australia |date=3 July 2016|accessdate=4 July 2016 }}</ref> 

The [[Australian Electoral Commission]] (AEC) has identified two seats that [[#Divisions in doubt|remain in doubt]], and reported that 76 seats have been won by the Liberal–National Coalition, while Labor has won 67 seats,<ref>http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-07-10/election-2016-malcolm-turnbull-claims-election-victory/7584400, at 2016-07-10 14:09</ref> which left one seat each to the [[Australian Greens]], [[Nick Xenophon Team]], [[Katter's Australian Party]], and [[Independent (politician)|independents]] [[Andrew Wilkie]] and [[Cathy McGowan (politician)|Cathy McGowan]].<ref name="abc results">{{cite web|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/federal-election-2016/results/ |title=Federal Election 2016 Results |date=3 July 2016 |accessdate=3 July 2016 |work=Australia Votes |publisher=ABC}}</ref>

The results in the [[Australian Senate]] were not all known by 11 July, and may remain unconfirmed for weeks. The voting system changed from full-preference [[single transferable vote]] with [[group voting ticket]]s to an [[Optional preferential voting|optional-preferential]] system in 2016.<ref name="smh senate">{{cite web |url=http://www.smh.com.au/federal-politics/federal-election-2016/election-2016-results-senate-count-throws-up-a-wild-mix-as-one-nation-fred-nile-liberal-democrats-vie-for-seats-20160703-gpxc8r.html|title=Election 2016 results: Senate count throws up a wild mix as One Nation, Fred Nile, Liberal Democrats vie for seats |author=Nicole Hasham |publisher=news.com.au |date=3 July 2016|accessdate=3 July 2016 }}</ref> The ABC's [[Antony Green]] has shown that the Coalition and Labor have won a minimum of 29 and 27 Senate seats respectively, and predicted that the [[Australian Greens|Greens]], [[Nick Xenophon Team]], [[Pauline Hanson's One Nation]], the [[Jacqui Lambie Network]], and [[Derryn Hinch's Justice Party]] were all likely to win one or more Senate seats each.<ref name="abc senate results">{{cite web|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/federal-election-2016/results/senate/ |title=Federal Election 2016: Senate Results |date=3 July 2016 |accessdate=4 July 2016 |work=Australia Votes |publisher=ABC}}</ref>

