==Electoral system==
[[Elections in Australia]] use a [[Full-preferential voting|full-preferential]] system in [[one vote, one value]] [[Single-winner voting system|single-member seats]] for the 150-member [[Australian House of Representatives|House of Representatives]] ([[lower house]]) and is changing from full-preferential [[group voting ticket]]s to an [[Optional preferential voting|optional-preferential]] [[single transferable vote]] system of [[proportional representation]] in the 76-member [[Australian Senate|Senate]] ([[upper house]]). Voting is [[compulsory voting|compulsory]], by [[Westminster system|Westminster]] convention, but subject to [[Constitution of Australia|constitutional]] constraints. The decision as to the type of election and its date is for the [[Prime Minister of Australia|prime minister]], who advises the [[Governor-General of Australia|governor-general]] to set the process in motion by dissolving the lower or both houses and issuing [[Writ of election|writs for election]].

==Election date==
Section 13 of the [[Constitution of Australia|constitution]] requires that in half-Senate elections the election of state senators must take place within one year before the places become vacant. Since the normal terms of half the senators would have ended on 30 June 2017, the writs for a half-Senate election could not be issued earlier than 1 July 2016, and the earliest possible date for a simultaneous House/half-Senate election would have been 6 August 2016.<ref name=GreenTimetable>{{cite news|last=Green|first=Antony|authorlink=Antony Green|title=Timetable for the Next Federal Election|url=http://blogs.abc.net.au/antonygreen/2013/11/timetable-for-next-federal-election.html|accessdate=13 November 2013|newspaper=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|location=Australia|date=12 November 2013}}</ref> There is no constitutional requirement for simultaneous elections for the Senate and the House of Representatives, and there are precedents for separate elections; however, governments and the electorate have long preferred that elections for the two Houses take place simultaneously.

A House-only election can be called at any time during a parliamentary term. Whether held simultaneously with a Senate election or separately, an election for the House of Representatives was required to have been held on or before 14 January 2017,<ref name=GreenTimetable /> which is calculated under provisions of the constitution and the [[Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918]] (CEA). [[Section 28 of the Constitution of Australia]] provides that the term of a House expires three years from the first sitting of the House, unless it is dissolved earlier. The [[Australian federal election, 2013|previous federal election]] was held on 7 September 2013. The 44th Parliament of Australia opened on 12 November 2013<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.aph.gov.au/About_Parliament/Sitting_Calendar/2013_Sitting_Calendar |title=2013 Parliamentary Sittings |publisher=[[Parliament of Australia]] |date= |accessdate= }}</ref> and its term would have expired on 11 November 2016.<ref>See [http://blogs.abc.net.au/antonygreen/2013/07/election-dates-and-extended-three-year-terms.html Antony Green's Election Blog]</ref> Writs for an election can be issued up to ten days after a dissolution or expiry of the House.<ref>[http://www.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/cea1918233/s32.html Commonwealth Electoral Act, s. 32]</ref> Up to 27 days can be allowed for nominations,<ref name="Commonwealth Electoral Act, s. 156">[http://www.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/cea1918233/s156.html Commonwealth Electoral Act, s. 156]</ref> and the actual election can be set for a maximum of 31 days after close of nominations,<ref name="Commonwealth Electoral Act, s. 157">[http://www.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/cea1918233/s157.html Commonwealth Electoral Act, s. 157]</ref> resulting in the latest possible House of Representatives election date of Saturday, 14 January 2017.

A [[double dissolution]] cannot take place within six months before the date of the expiry of the House of Representatives.{{efn|Section 57 of the Constitution}} That meant that a double dissolution could not be granted after 11 May 2016. Allowing for the same stages indicated above, the last possible date for a double dissolution election was 16 July 2016.<ref name=GreenTimetable />

On 2 November 2015, Prime Minister [[Malcolm Turnbull]] stated: "I would say around September–October [2016] is when you should expect the next election to be."<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.theage.com.au/federal-politics/political-news/malcolm-turnbulls-year-of-living-courageously-20151102-gkozge.html|title=Malcolm Turnbull's year of living courageously|work=[[The Age]] |date=2 November 2015 |first=Mark|last=Kenny}}</ref> However, in December 2015, the [[Australian Broadcasting Corporation|ABC]] reported that some "senior Liberal MPs" had been seeking an election as early as March 2016.<ref>{{cite web|title=What are the implications for Briggs, Brough and Turnbull?|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2015-12-30/barnes-two-resignations-and-a-political-funeral/7059148|website=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC]] The Drum|accessdate=4 January 2016}}</ref> An election held at this time would have required a separate half-Senate election to be held in late 2016 or early 2017.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Green|first1=Antony|title=The Turnbull Government's Options for a 2016 Election|url=http://blogs.abc.net.au/antonygreen/2015/12/the-turnbull-governments-options-for-a-2016-election.html#more|website=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |accessdate=4 January 2016}}</ref>

On 21 March 2016, Turnbull announced that the parliament would be recalled for both houses to sit on 18 April to consider for a third time the bills to reinstate the [[Office of the Australian Building and Construction Commissioner|Australian Building and Construction Commission]] (ABCC). Turnbull also said that if the Senate failed to pass the bill, then there would be a double dissolution of parliament and an election would be held on 2 July. The delivery of the [[Australian federal budget, 2016|federal budget]] was also brought forward from 10 May to 3 May.<ref name="aprilrecall">{{cite news|title=Malcolm Turnbull recalls Parliament for April 18 sitting ahead of early election|url=http://www.smh.com.au/federal-politics/political-news/malcolm-turnbull-recalls-parliament-for-april-18-sitting-ahead-of-early-election-20160320-gnmy2v.html|accessdate=21 March 2016|work=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]]|date=21 March 2016}}</ref> On 18 April, the Senate once again rejected the bills to reinstate the ABCC. On 8 May Malcolm Turnbull attended [[Government House, Canberra|Government House]] to advise the [[Governor-General of Australia|Governor-General]] to issue the writs for a double dissolution on 9 May. This confirmed the date of the election; 2 July 2016.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.9news.com.au/National/2016/05/08/08/46/Prime-Minister-Malcolm-Turnbull-set-to-seek-double-dissolution-election|title=It's official: Malcolm Turnbull confirms Australia will go to the polls on July 2|work=Ninemsn|date=8 May 2016}}</ref>

The [[Northern Territory general election, 2016|2016 Northern Territory election]] will occur in the following month.

===Double dissolution triggers===
By 18 April 2016 there were four bills that met the requirements of [[Section 57 of the Constitution of Australia|Section 57 of the constitution]] for a [[double dissolution]].<ref>[http://www.aph.gov.au/Parliamentary_Business/Statistics/Senate_StatsNet/legislation/triggers44 Bills laid aside or negatived, their history and status as possibly meeting the requirements of section 57 of the Constitution (44th Parliament – 1st and 2nd sessions)]. Retrieved 20 April 2016</ref> 
