On 19 April the Prime Minister confirmed that, following the 2016 federal budget set for 3 May, he would advise the Governor-General to call a double dissolution election on Saturday 2 July. Any or all of these four bills could have been cited in his advice. On Sunday 8 May 2016, Turnbull visited [[Government House, Canberra|Government House]] and formally advised the Governor-General to dissolve both Houses of Parliament and [[Writ of election|issue the writs]] for a double dissolution election to be held on 2 July 2016. The advice was based on Parliament's inability to pass the following three bills:<ref name="timing101">{{cite web|url=https://www.gg.gov.au/sites/default/files/files/IPS/Documents%20relating%20to%20calling%20of%20the%20double%20dissolution%20election.pdf|title=DOCUMENTS RELATING TO THE CALLING OF THE DOUBLE DISSOLUTION ELECTION FOR 2 JULY 2016|work=Governor-General of the Commonwealth of Australia|date=8 May 2016|archiveurl=http://web.archive.org/web/20160508072551/https://www.gg.gov.au/sites/default/files/files/IPS/Documents%20relating%20to%20calling%20of%20the%20double%20dissolution%20election.pdf|archivedate=8 May 2016}}</ref>
* ''Building and Construction Industry (Consequential and Transitional Provisions) Bill 2013''
* ''Building and Construction Industry (Improving Productivity) Bill 2013''
* ''Fair Work (Registered Organisations) Amendment Bill 2014''.

The Governor-General accepted the advice and dissolved the Parliament the following day, 9 May 2016.

===Constitutional and legal provisions===
The constitutional and legal provisions which impacted on the choice of election dates include:<ref>{{cite web |author=Lundie, Rob |url=http://www.aph.gov.au/library/pubs/BN/2008-09/aust_elections.htm |title=Australian elections timetable |publisher=[[Parliament of Australia]] |date= |accessdate= }}</ref>

*[[Section 12 of the Constitution of Australia|Section 12 of the Constitution]] provides that: "The Governor of any State may cause writs to be issued for the election of Senators for the State."
*[[Section 13 of the Constitution of Australia|Section 13 of the Constitution]] provides that the election of Senators shall be held in the period of twelve months before the places become vacant.
*Section 28 of the Constitution provides that: "Every House of Representatives shall continue for three years from the first sitting of the House, and no longer, but may be sooner dissolved by the Governor-General."<ref name="section28">[http://www.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/coaca430/s28.html Commonwealth Of Australia Constitution Act – Section 28]</ref> Since the 44th Parliament opened on 12 November 2013, it will expire on 11 November 2016.{{efn|The reason it does not expire on 12 November 2016 is because 12 November 2013 was "Day 1" of the current House, not "Day 0". Therefore 12 November 2016 would be "Year 3, Day 1"; if the House sat on this day, it would be serving for longer than its 3-year mandate. Therefore its term would expire on the previous day. See Antony Green's Election Blog}}
*[[Section 32 of the Constitution of Australia|Section 32 of the Constitution]] provides that: "The writs shall be issued within ten days from the expiry of a House of Representatives or from the proclamation of a dissolution thereof." Ten days after 11 November 2016 is 21 November 2016.
*Section 156 (1) of the [[Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918]] (CEA) provides that: "The date fixed for the nomination of the candidates shall not be less than 10 days nor more than 27 days after the date of the writ".<ref name="Commonwealth Electoral Act, s. 156"/> Twenty-seven days after 21 November 2016 is 18 December 2016.
*Section 157 of the CEA provides that: "The date fixed for the polling shall not be less than 23 days nor more than 31 days after the date of nomination".<ref name="Commonwealth Electoral Act, s. 157"/> Thirty-one days after 18 December 2016 is 18 January 2017, a Wednesday.
*Section 158 of the CEA provides that: "The day fixed for the polling shall be a Saturday".<ref>[http://www.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/cea1918233/s158.html Commonwealth Electoral Act, s. 158]</ref> The Saturday before 18 January 2017 is 14 January 2017. This was therefore the latest possible date for the election.  However, it was unlikely that an election would have been called for this date, as schools would be closed for summer holidays at this time.  Governments tend to avoid holding elections during school holidays, since schools are often used as polling places.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://blogs.abc.net.au/antonygreen/2010/07/possible-federal-election-date.html |title=Possible federal election dates |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |author=Green, Antony |authorlink=Antony Green |date=July 2010 |accessdate= }}</ref>

==Election timeline==
On 8 May 2016, the office of the Governor-General released documents relating to the calling of the election. The documents set out a timeline of key dates for the election.<ref name="timing101"/>
* 9 May – Dissolution of both houses of the Parliament of Australia<ref name="timing101"/>
* 16 May – Issue of writs<ref name="timing101"/>
* 23 May – Close of electoral rolls<ref name="timing101"/>
* 9 June – Close of candidate nominations<ref name="timing101"/>
* 2 July – Polling day<ref name="timing101"/>
* 8 August – Return of writs<ref name="timing101"/> (last day)

==Background==
<!--{{see also|Mackerras pendulum for the next Australian federal election}} please update NSW and WA before restoring link -->
The [[Coalition (Australia)|Coalition]] won the [[Australian federal election, 2013|2013 federal election]] with 90 of the 150 seats in the House of Representatives, on a swing of 17 seats or 3.6 points on a [[Two-party-preferred vote|two-party]] basis, defeating the six-year [[Australian Labor Party|Labor]] government. Labor held 55 seats, and [[crossbenchers]] held the remaining five.<ref name="2pp">{{cite web |url=http://results.aec.gov.au/17496/Website/HouseStateFirstPrefsByParty-17496-NAT.htm |title=First house preference by party |work=Virtual Tally Room: 2013 election |publisher=[[Australian Electoral Commission]] (AEC) |date=4 November 2013|accessdate=28 November 2013 }}</ref> The [[Abbott government]] was sworn in to office on 18 September 2013.<ref>{{cite news|title=Abbott's team to be sworn in next week|url=http://news.theage.com.au/breaking-news-national/abbotts-team-to-be-sworn-in-next-week-20130909-2tepy.html|accessdate=13 September 2013|newspaper=[[The Age]]|date=9 September 2013}}</ref>

[[Kevin Rudd]] resigned as Labor's leader following the defeat of the party. [[Chris Bowen]] became the interim leader in the approach to a [[Australian Labor Party leadership spill, October 2013|leadership election]]. Two candidates, [[Anthony Albanese]] and [[Bill Shorten]], declared their candidacy; Shorten was declared the winner on 13 October 2013.<ref>{{cite news|title=Bill Shorten elected Labor leader|url=http://www.smh.com.au/federal-politics/political-news/bill-shorten-elected-labor-leader-20131013-2vfzy.html|accessdate=13 October 2013|newspaper=Sydney Morning Herald|author=Harrison, Dan}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2013-10-13/bill-shorten-elected-labor-leader/5019116|author=Griffiths, Emma|title=Bill Shorten elected Labor leader over Anthony Albanese after month-long campaign|work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|location=Australia|date=13 October 2013|accessdate=15 October 2013}}</ref>

As a result of lost ballot papers, on 18 February 2014 the [[High Court of Australia]], sitting as the [[Court of Disputed Returns (Australia)|Court of Disputed Returns]], ordered a [[Australian Senate special election in Western Australia, 2014|new half-Senate election]] for Western Australia, which was held on 5 April 2014.<ref name=SBS>{{cite news|url=http://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/2014/02/20/fresh-wa-poll-throws-canberra-spin|title=Fresh WA poll throws Canberra into spin |agency=AAP |work=SBS Online |date=20 February 2014 |accessdate=22 February 2014}}</ref><ref name=decision>{{cite AustLII|litigants=Australian Electoral Commission v Johnston|source=HCA |num=5 |year=2014 |date=18 February 2014 |courtname=H.C.}}</ref>

Senator [[John Madigan (Australian politician)|John Madigan]] resigned from the [[Democratic Labour Party (Australia)|Democratic Labour Party]] and became an [[Independent (politics)|independent]] Senator in September 2014, citing long-term internal party tensions.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2014-09-04/senator-madigan-cuts-ties-with-democratic-labour-party/5718776 |title=Senator Madigan cuts ties with Democratic Labour Party, will serve out term as independent |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |date=4 September 2014 |accessdate= |author= }}</ref>

On 24 November 2014, Tasmanian Senator [[Jacqui Lambie]] resigned from the [[Palmer United Party]] and on 13 March 2015, Queensland Senator [[Glenn Lazarus]] also announced his resignation from the Palmer United Party; both continue to sit as independents.{{citation needed|date=July 2016}}

On 14 September 2015, the incumbent Prime Minister, [[Tony Abbott]], was [[Liberal Party of Australia leadership spill, September 2015|challenged for the leadership]] of the [[Liberal Party of Australia|Liberal Party]] and thus the prime ministership by Malcolm Turnbull, the Minister for Communications. Turnbull won the vote 54–44 and on 15 September was sworn in as prime minister, starting the [[Turnbull government]]. On 11 February 2016 [[Warren Truss]], the [[Deputy Prime Minister of Australia|Deputy Prime Minister]], announced his decision to retire from politics at the 2016 federal election and immediately stood aside as Leader of the [[National Party of Australia|Nationals]]. [[Barnaby Joyce]] was elected as Leader and was sworn as the Deputy Prime Minister, and [[Fiona Nash]] was elected as Deputy Leader of the Nationals.<ref name="theguardian1">{{cite news |url=http://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2016/feb/11/warren-truss-to-reveal-his-retirement-plans-politics-live?page=with:block-56bc5be2e4b072fd3ded30f5#block-56bc5be2e4b072fd3ded30f5 |title=Barnaby Joyce wins Nationals leadership, Fiona Nash named deputy |work=[[The Guardian]] |location=Australia |author=Murphy, Katharine |date=11 February 2016 |accessdate=11 February 2016 }}</ref><ref name="autogenerated1">{{cite news |url=http://www.smh.com.au/federal-politics/political-news/parliament-pays-tribute-to-retiring-deputy-pm-warren-truss-ahead-of-barnaby-joyce-elevation-20160210-gmr3ca.html |title=Parliament pays tribute to retiring deputy PM Warren Truss ahead of Barnaby Joyce elevation |work=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]] |author=Gartrell, Adam |date=11 February 2016 |accessdate=11 February 2016 }}</ref><ref name="autogenerated2">{{cite news |url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-02-11/barnaby-joyce-named-as-new-nationals-leader/7161584 |title=Barnaby Joyce elected unopposed as new Nationals leader |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |author=Keany, Francis |date=11 February 2016 |accessdate= 11 February 2016 }}</ref> Truss also resigned from the Turnbull Ministry.<ref name=Age130216>{{cite news |url=http://www.theage.com.au/federal-politics/political-news/cabinet-reshuffle-malcolm-turnbull-announces-new-frontbench-as-mal-brough-resigns-20160213-gmta7z.html |title=Cabinet reshuffle: Malcolm Turnbull announces new frontbench as Mal Brough resigns |work=[[The Age]] |date=13 February 2016 |accessdate=13 February 2016 |author=Massola, James}}</ref>

[[Ian Macfarlane (politician)|Ian Macfarlane]] attempted to defect from the Liberal to the National party room with accompanying demands for additional Nationals cabinet representation, and the [[Mal Brough]]–James Ashby diary controversy deepened in the last week of the campaign.<ref name=ianmc>{{cite news |url=http://www.smh.com.au/federal-politics/political-news/liberal-mp-ian-macfarlane-defects-to-nationals-in-shock-move-against-malcolm-turnbull-20151203-gle6rd.html |title=Liberal MP Ian Macfarlane defects to Nationals in shock move against Malcolm Turnbull |author=Massola, James |work=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]] |date=3 December 2015 |accessdate= }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.businessinsider.com.au/why-taking-an-overseas-trip-spells-trouble-for-malcolm-turnbull-2015-12  |title=Malcolm Turnbull suddenly has a lot of problems |work=Business Insider |date=3 December 2015 |accessdate= }}</ref> Along with the unexpected by-election swing and Turnbull's significantly lessened personal ratings in the concurrent December Newspoll, some [[News Limited]] journalists claimed that Turnbull's honeymoon was over.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.switzer.com.au/the-experts/david-speers/the-honeymoon-is-over/ |title=The honeymoon is over |author=Speers, David |work=Switzer |date=3 December 2015 |accessdate= }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.couriermail.com.au/news/opinion/party-games-turnbulls-honeymoon-coming-to-an-end/news-story/74e526f974eb2be4f52f085aa311106e |title=Turnbull's 'honeymoon' coming to an end |work=[[Courier-Mail]] |date=3 December 2015 |accessdate= }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.heraldsun.com.au/news/opinion/honeymoon-over-for-malcolm-turnbull/news-story/10644e38e9a90f497296696ae4248f47 |title=Honeymoon over for Malcolm Turnbull |work=[[Herald Sun]] |date=6 December 2015 |accessdate= }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.couriermail.com.au/news/opinion/opinion-is-a-13-per-cent-swing-against-government-in-north-sydney-a-warning-for-malcolm-turnbull/news-story/fb4ca4201b4e0bcf7dab36f87f8f1aaa |title=Is the Turnbull honeymoon over? |work=[[Courier-Mail]] |date=7 December 2015 |accessdate= }}</ref><ref name=brough>{{cite news |url=http://www.adelaidenow.com.au/business/breaking-news/turnbull-ducks-talk-on-broughs-future/story-fnn9c0gw-1227637192122 |title=Turnbull ducks talk on Brough's future |work=[[The Advertiser (Adelaide)|The Advertiser]] |date=8 December 2015 |accessdate= }}</ref>

The [[Nick Xenophon Team]] (NXT) listed many [[Candidates of the Australian federal election, 2016|candidates throughout Australia]],<ref>{{cite web |url=https://nxt.org.au/candidates/ |title=2016 NXT candidates |publisher=[[Nick Xenophon Team]] |date= |accessdate= }}</ref> with [[Australian Broadcasting Corporation|ABC]] [[psephologist]] [[Antony Green]] indicating NXT has a "strong chance of winning lower house seats and three or four Senate seats".<ref name=sa2016abcpreview>{{cite news |url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/federal-election-2016/guide/preview-sa/ |title=Election Guide (SA) |work=2016 federal election guide |author=Green, Antony |authorlink=Antony Green |publisher=[[Australian Broadcasting Commission|ABC]] |date=2016 |accessdate= }}</ref>

Liberal Senator [[Michael Ronaldson]] announced on 18 December 2015 that he would leave parliament before the next election, after moving from the outer ministry in the [[Abbott government]] to the backbench in the Turnbull government. He resigned on 28 February 2016, and the [[Casual vacancies in the Australian Parliament|casual vacancy]] was filled by [[James Paterson (Australian politician)|James Paterson]] on 9 March 2016.<ref name="paterson">{{cite web|title=Mr James Paterson was appointed to the @ausenate seat vacated by the Hon Michael Ronaldson|url=https://twitter.com/VicParliament/status/707475106587615232|website=Twitter|publisher=@VicParliament|accessdate=9 March 2016}}</ref>

Labor Senator [[Joe Bullock]] announced on 1 March 2016 that he would be resigning from the Senate after the autumn sittings of parliament, citing—among other reasons—his opposition to [[same-sex marriage]] being at odds with the Labor Party's platform to bind its members in a supporting vote after 2019.<ref name="bullock">{{cite news|title=Joe Bullock, Labor senator, announces retirement from politics over party position on same-sex marriage|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-03-01/labor-senator-joe-bullock-quits/7212324|accessdate=1 March 2016|work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|location=Australia |date=1 March 2016 |author1=Borrello, Eliza |author2=Doran, Matthew }}</ref> Bullock tendered his resignation to Senate President Stephen Parry on 13 April 2016.<ref>{{cite news|title=Bullock sends a history lesson|url=https://au.news.yahoo.com/thewest/a/31358325/retiring-labor-joe-bullock-sends-a-history-lesson|accessdate=16 April 2016|date=15 April 2016 |work=[[Yahoo7]] |location=Australia }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.aph.gov.au/Senators_and_Members/Parliamentarian?MPID=243237 |title=Former Senator Joe Bullock |work=Senators and Members |publisher=[[Parliament of Australia]] |date= |accessdate= }}</ref> The casual vacancy was filled by [[Pat Dodson]] on 28 April 2016.<ref name="dodson">{{cite news|title=Pat Dodson elected WA's newest senator|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-04-28/pat-dodson-endorsed-as-wa-senate-candidate/7366640|accessdate=29 April 2016|work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|location=Australia|date=28 April 2016|author=Perpitch, Nicolas}}</ref>

Palmer United Party leader [[Clive Palmer]] announced on 4 May 2016 that he would not recontest his seat of [[Division of Fairfax|Fairfax]] at the election.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-05-04/clive-palmer-will-not-see-reelection-of-house-of-representative/7382498|title=Clive Palmer will not seek re-election in House of Representatives|work=ABC News|date=4 May 2016}}</ref> On 23 May he also ruled out running for a Senate seat.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.9news.com.au/National/2016/05/23/11/27/Clive-Palmer-rules-out-Senate-seat-run|title='Everyone was out to get me': Palmer|date=23 May 2016|work=Ninemsn}}</ref>

In the lower house seat of [[Division of Brisbane|Brisbane]], both Liberal-National and Labor candidates were [[openly gay]]; a first in Australian federal political history.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.smh.com.au/federal-politics/political-news/trevor-evans-wins-brisbane-lnp-preselection-setting-up-historic-election-20160414-go6pzh.html |title=Brisbane – 2016 election |author=Green, Antony |authorlink=Antony Green |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |date= |accessdate= }}</ref>

==By-elections==
Rudd resigned from parliament on 22 November 2013, triggering the [[Griffith by-election, 2014|2014 Griffith by-election]], which was held on 8 February, with [[Terri Butler]] retaining the seat for Labor.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://results.aec.gov.au/17552/Website/HouseDivisionFirstPrefs-17552-163.htm|title=The official election results|website=Griffith by-election 2014|publisher=[[Australian Electoral Commission]]|date=27 February 2014|accessdate=30 March 2016}}</ref>

On 21 July 2015, Liberal [[Don Randall]] died, triggering the [[Canning by-election, 2015|2015 Canning by-election]], which was held on 19 September. [[Andrew Hastie (politician)|Andrew Hastie]] retained the seat for the Liberals, having to rely on preferences after suffering a substantial [[Swing (Australian politics)|swing]] to the Labor candidate.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2015-09-20/canning-by-election-both-major-parties-hail-result/6789540 |title=Liberal and Labor parties hail success of campaigns |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |date=20 September 2015 |accessdate= |author= }}</ref>

[[Joe Hockey]] was not retained as Treasurer in Turnbull's cabinet, and announced his resignation from parliament shortly afterwards, triggering the [[North Sydney by-election, 2015|2015 North Sydney by-election]] which was held on 5 December. The seat was retained for the Liberal Party by [[Trent Zimmerman]]. Zimmerman won with 48.2% of the primary vote after a larger-than-predicted 12.8-point swing against the Turnbull Coalition government.  This was only the second time in North Sydney since federation that the successful Liberal candidate failed to obtain a majority of the primary vote and had to rely on preferences. Zimmerman faced a double-digit primary vote swing − more than triple that of the [[Canning by-election, 2015|2015 Canning by-election]] − despite the absence of a Labor candidate. Labor has never been successful in the safe Liberal seat. The Liberal [[Two-party-preferred vote|two-candidate]] vote of 60.2% against [[Independent politician|independent]] Stephen Ruff compares with the previous election vote of 65.9% against Labor.<ref>[http://www.abc.net.au/news/elections/north-sydney-by-election-2015/ 2015 North Sydney by-election: Antony Green ABC]</ref> The reduction of 5.7 points cannot be considered a "two-party/candidate preferred [[Swing (Australian politics)|swing]]" − when a major party is absent, preference flows to both major parties do not take place, resulting in asymmetric preference flows.<ref name=asymmetric1>{{cite web|url=http://blogs.abc.net.au/antonygreen/2012/02/a-comment-on-the-size-of-the-port-adelaide-swing.html |title=A Comment on the Size of the Port Adelaide Swing |author=Green, Antony |authorlink=Antony Green |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |date=13 February 2012 |accessdate=26 July 2012}}</ref><ref name=asymmetric2>{{cite web |url=http://blogs.abc.net.au/antonygreen/2011/05/an-example-of-non-monotonicity-and-opportunites-for-tactical-voting-at-an-australian-election.html |title=An Example of Non-Monotonicity and Opportunites for Tactical Voting at an Australian Election |author=Green, Antony |authorlink=Antony Green |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |date=4 May 2011 |accessdate= }}</ref>

==Redistributions and name changes==
In November 2014 the [[Australian Electoral Commission]] announced that a redistribution of electoral boundaries in New South Wales and Western Australia would be undertaken before the next election. A determination of the states' membership entitlements under the Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918 means that Western Australia's entitlement would increase from 15 to 16 seats, and New South Wales' would decrease from 48 to 47 seats. A redistribution also occurred in the Australian Capital Territory, as seven years elapsed since the last time the ACT's boundaries were reviewed.<ref name=nswwa>{{cite news|title=Determination of membership entitlement to the House of Representatives|url=http://www.aec.gov.au/media/media-releases/2014/11-13.htm|accessdate=14 November 2014|publisher=[[Australian Electoral Commission]]|date=13 November 2014}}</ref> On 16 November 2015, the AEC announced that a redistribution of electoral boundaries in Tasmania would be deferred until after the next election, as the Electoral Act provides that a redistribution shall not commence where there is less than a year until the expiry of the House of Representatives (i.e., 11 November 2016).<ref name=aectasredis>{{cite web|title=Deferral of the redistribution of federal electoral boundaries in Tasmania|url=http://www.aec.gov.au/media/media-releases/2015/11-16.htm|publisher=[[Australian Electoral Commission]]|accessdate=16 November 2015}}</ref>

In October 2015 the AEC announced plans to abolish the [[Division of Hunter|seat of Hunter]]. Electors in the north of Hunter would have joined [[Division of New England|New England]], while the roughly 40% remainder would have become part of [[Division of Paterson|Paterson]], where the Liberal margin would have been reduced from a fairly safe 9.8 points to an extremely marginal 0.5 point as a result. Hunter was first contested at the [[Australian federal election, 1901|inaugural 1901 federal election]]; the AEC's naming guidelines require it to make "every effort" to preserve the names of the original federal divisions.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.aec.gov.au/Electorates/Redistributions/guidelines.htm |publisher=[[Australian Electoral Commission]] |title=Guidelines for Naming Federal Electoral Divisions |date= |accessdate=16 November 2015 }}</ref> The commission proposed renaming [[Division of Charlton|Charlton]] to Hunter, and in honour of deceased Prime Minister [[Gough Whitlam]], renaming [[Division of Throsby|Throsby]] to [[Division of Whitlam|Whitlam]].<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2015-10-16/electoral-commission-to-abolish-federal-nsw-seat-of-hunter/6860172 |title=Australian Electoral Commission to abolish Federal NSW seat of Hunter |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |date=16 October 2015 |accessdate= |author= }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://blogs.crikey.com.au/pollbludger/2015/10/16/draft-federal-redistribution-of-new-south-wales/ |title=Draft federal redistribution of New South Wales |work=[[Crikey]]: Poll Bludger |date=16 October 2015 |accessdate= }}</ref>

The final proposal, however, saw Charlton abolished, with Hunter moving slightly eastward to take in much of Charlton's territory.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/federal-election-2016/guide/hunt/ |title=Hunter – Australia Votes &#124; Federal Election 2016 (Australian Broadcasting Corporation) |publisher=Abc.net.au |date= |accessdate=2016-06-15}}</ref> Additionally, Paterson was made more compact and pushed well to the south, taking in some heavily Labor territory that had previously been in Hunter and [[Division of Newcastle|Newcastle]]. This had the effect of erasing the Liberals' majority in Paterson; Labor now has a notional majority of 0.5 percent.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/federal-election-2016/guide/pate/ |title=Paterson – Australia Votes &#124; Federal Election 2016  |publisher=[[Australian Broadcasting Corporation]] |date= |accessdate=15 June 2016}}</ref>

The new seat created in Western Australia was the [[Division of Burt]], named after the Burt family, and centred on the south-east areas of metropolitan Perth.<ref name="aecburt">{{cite web|title=Profile of the electoral division of Burt (WA)|url=http://www.aec.gov.au/profiles/wa/burt.htm|publisher=Australian Electoral Commission|accessdate=9 May 2016}}</ref>

The [[Division of Fraser]] in the ACT was renamed [[Division of Fenner]], to honour the late scientist Professor [[Frank Fenner]]. The AEC announced that the name Fraser will be used for a future division in Victoria, named in honour of [[Malcolm Fraser]], a former Prime Minister.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.aec.gov.au/profiles/act/fenner.htm |publisher=[[Australian Electoral Commission]] |title=Division of Fenner |accessdate=21 April 2016 }}</ref>

==Senate voting changes==
Following the previous election, the [[Abbott Government]] announced it would investigate changing the electoral system for the [[Australian Senate|Senate]]. On 22 February 2016, the [[Turnbull Government]] announced several proposed changes.<ref name="reforms">{{cite news |url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-02-22/senate-election-reforms-announced-including-preferential-voting/7188792 |title=Senate election reforms announced, including preferential voting above the line |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |date=22 February 2016 |accessdate= |author=Borrello, Eliza }}</ref> In the Senate, the changes had the support of the [[Coalition (Australia)|Liberal/National Coalition]], the [[Australian Greens]], and [[Nick Xenophon]] − a three-vote majority.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://theconversation.com/explainer-what-changes-to-the-senate-voting-system-are-being-proposed-55128 |title=Explainer: what changes to the Senate voting system are being proposed? |author=Morey, Stephen |work=[[The Conversation (website)|The Conversation]] |date=23 February 2016 |accessdate= }}</ref> The legislation passed both houses of the [[Parliament of Australia]] on 18 March 2016 after the Senate sat all night debating the bill.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-03-18/senate-electoral-reform-laws-passed/7258212 |title=Electoral laws passed after marathon Parliament sitting |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |date=18 March 2016 |author=Borrello, Eliza }}</ref>

The changes abolished [[group voting ticket]]s (GVTs) and introduced ''optional'' preferential voting, along with party logos on the ballot paper. The ballot paper continues to have a box for each party above a heavy line, with each party's candidates in a column below that party's box below the solid line. Previously, a voter could either mark a single box ''above the line'', which triggered the party's group voting ticket (a pre-assigned sequence of preferences), or place a number in every box ''below the line'' to assign their own preferences. As a result of the changes, voters may assign their preferences for parties above the line (numbering as many boxes as they wish), or individual candidates below the line, and are not required to fill all of the boxes. Both above and below the line voting are now [[optional preferential voting]]. For above the line, voters will be instructed to write at least their first six preferences; however, a "savings provision" will still count the ballot if less than six were given. As a result, fewer votes are expected to be classed as informal; however, more ballots will "exhaust" as a result (i.e. some votes are not counted towards electing any candidate). For below the line, voters will be required to write at least their first 12 preferences. Voters will be free to continue numbering as many preferences as they like beyond the minimum number specified. Another savings provision will allow ballot papers with at least 6 below the line preferences to be formal, catering for people who confuse the above and below the line instructions.

[[Antony Green]], a [[psephology|psephologist]] for the [[Australian Broadcasting Corporation]], wrote several publications on various aspects of the proposed Senate changes.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://blogs.abc.net.au/antonygreen/2016/03/senate-reform-below-the-line-optional-preferential-voting.html |title=Senate Reform – Below the Line Optional Preferential Voting Included in Government's Legislation |author=Green, Antony |authorlink=Antony Green |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |date=2 March 2016 |accessdate= }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://blogs.abc.net.au/antonygreen/2016/02/would-electoral-reform-deliver-the-coalition-a-senate-majority-at-a-double-dissolution.html |title=Would Electoral Reform Deliver the Coalition a Senate Majority at a Double Dissolution? |author=Green, Antony |authorlink=Antony Green |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |date=17 February 2016 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://blogs.abc.net.au/antonygreen/2015/09/the-orogins-of-senate-group-ticket-voting-and-it-wasnt-the-major-parties.html |title=The Origin of Senate Group Ticket Voting, and it didn't come from the Major Parties |author=Green, Antony |authorlink=Antony Green |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |date=23 September 2015 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://blogs.abc.net.au/antonygreen/2015/06/the-likely-political-consequences-of-changing-the-senates-electoral-system.html |title=The Likely Political Consequences of Proposed Changes to the Senate's Electoral System |author=Green, Antony |authorlink=Antony Green |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |date=24 June 2015 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://blogs.abc.net.au/antonygreen/2015/06/by-accident-rather-than-design-a-brief-history-fo-the-senates-electoral-system.html |title=By Accident Rather than Design – a Brief History of the Senate's Electoral System |author=Green, Antony |authorlink=Antony Green |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |date=10 June 2015 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://blogs.abc.net.au/antonygreen/senate-elections/ |title=Senate Elections|author=Green, Antony |authorlink=Antony Green |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |location=Australia |date=25 April 2016 }}</ref> As with every double-dissolution election the entire Senate is re-elected (12 seats per state rather than the usual half Senate election of six seats per state). Consequently, the number of votes required to earn a seat (the "quota") is halved. Due to the abolition of GVTs, it is no longer possible to create "calculators" that assess, with reasonable accuracy, the eventual senate election outcome. Therefore, according to Antony Green, "my working guide is that if a party has more than 0.5 of a quota, it will be in the race for one of the final seats." His calculation of the percentage of primary-vote required for the first six full- and half-quotas at this election is as follows:<ref>{{cite web|url=http://blogs.abc.net.au/antonygreen/2016/06/2016-senate-calculators.html|title=2016 Senate Calculator Plus Tips on the New Senate Voting System|website=Antony Green's Election Blog|access-date=2016-06-09}}</ref>
