The metropolitan dailies backed the Coalition without exception. Turnbull's personal qualities and platform appealed more to the editors than his short record in office. ''[[The Age]]'' spoke for many in concluding that "Mr Turnbull deserves the chance to deliver".<ref name=age-ed>{{cite news
| newspaper=The Age
| title=Turnbull needs more time to make his mark
| date=1 July 2016
| page=16
}}</ref> ''[[The Daily Telegraph (Australia)|The Daily Telegraph]]'' praised the Prime Minister's "consistency ... He has stuck fast on addressing Australia's debt and deficit problems."<ref name=tele-ed>{{cite news
| newspaper=The Daily Telegraph
| title=Nation needs steady course
| date=1 July 2016
| page=38
}}</ref> Newspapers particularly highlighted, in the words of the ''[[The West Australian|West Australian]]'', the Coalition's "better understanding of what is needed to run the economy and rein in debt."<ref name=west-ed>{{cite news
| newspaper=The West Australian
| title=Voting Liberal in both Houses best choice for stable future
| date=1 July 2016
| page=58
}}</ref> The ''[[Sydney Morning Herald]]'' welcomed the Coalition's curbs on superannuation tax breaks and offered qualified support for company tax cuts ("Businesses, small and large, create jobs.")<ref name=smh-ed>{{cite news
| newspaper=Sydney Morning Herald
| title=Turnbull Coalition offers economic, social reform
| date=1 July 2016
| page=16
}}</ref> ''The Age'' endorsed the super changes and company tax cut, but was critical of the Coalition's "shamefully harsh" border protection policies and planned same-sex marriage plebiscite.<ref name=age-ed /> The ''[[Canberra Times]]'' gave the Coalition "credit for recognising the necessity for cuts in public-sector spending" and reforming super.<ref name=ct-ed>{{cite news
| newspaper=Canberra Times
| title=Stronger, more effective government
| date=1 July 2016
| page=2
}}</ref> The ''[[The Courier-Mail|Courier-Mail]]'' backed Turnbull's 'jobs and growth' agenda as "a more coherent prescription in an era that demands experience, stability and certainty."<ref name=courier-ed>{{cite news
| newspaper=Courier-Mail
| title=Turnbull best bet to lead
| date=1 July 2016
| page=30
}}</ref>

Indeed, several editorials cited the need for political stability in an uncertain world: declining growth, the end of the mining boom, terrorism and external economic shocks. The Coalition was judged best-placed to provide that stability, either because of its incumbency, focus on growth, parliamentary numbers or comparative party unity.{{citation needed|date=July 2016}}

The ''Courier-Mail'', ''Canberra Times'', ''Age'' and Adelaide ''[[The Advertiser (Adelaide)|Advertiser]]'' acknowledged Shorten's strengths on the campaign trail. The ''Advertiser'' spoke of a "revitalised performance from Bill Shorten ... The Opposition is now in with a fighting chance."<ref name=tiser-ed>{{cite news
| newspaper=The Advertiser
| title=Turnbull the clear choice for these turbulent times
| date=1 July 2016
| page=20
}}</ref> The ''Age'' welcomed its plans on negative gearing and capital gains tax; the ''Herald'' backed the case for Labor's higher increases in education funding.

However, for almost every paper, this was outweighed by the party's service of union interests and its unsustainable profligacy. "There is no sign at all that Mr Shorten will force Labor to remove the disproportionate influence of unions and their money on his party," the ''Herald'' wrote, regretting that despite the reforming instincts of Shadow Treasurer [[Chris Bowen]], as a whole "Labor baulks at progress that affects the public service and unions." For the ''NT News'', "the party's policy positions are incoherent. The sheer breadth of spending promises show Labor is not ready for government." The ''[[Herald Sun]]'' agreed that in Labor's "[[DNA]] is an inclination to spend ... That Australia must learn to live within its means is a lesson Labor still fails to grasp."<ref name=hun-ed>{{cite news
| newspaper=Herald Sun
| title=Australia deserves a vote for stability
| date=1 July 2016
| page=34
}}</ref> The ''Herald'' chided Shorten for the dishonesty of his Medicare scare campaign ("his judgement was found wanting") as did the ''Courier-Mail'' ("hysteria"). The ''Daily Telegraph'' also highlighted Labor's record on border protection – 50,000 illegal maritime arrivals and perhaps 1,000 deaths at sea – and noted the widespread desire of many Labor candidates and the Greens to "attempt this deadly experiment again."

Local issues and candidates also played a role in the endorsements. Expecting a Coalition victory, the ''[[Northern Territory News|NT News]]'' argued that [[Division of Solomon|Solomon]] [[Country Liberal Party|Country Liberal]] MP [[Natasha Griggs]] should be returned on the grounds that Darwin was better off having a Government member as its representative.<ref name=nt-ed>{{cite news
| newspaper=NT News
| title=Why you must vote Coalition
| date=1 July 2016
| page=40
}}</ref> Victoria's ''Herald Sun'' highlighted the [[Country Fire Authority]] dispute – "a window into the relationship between Labor and the union movement" and "a game changer in how many Victorians will vote."<ref name=hun-ed /> The Hobart ''Mercury'' concluded that although the nation would be "best served" by a Coalition government, [[Division of Denison|Denison]] independent MP [[Andrew Wilkie]] and [[Division of Franklin|Franklin]] Labor MP [[Julie Collins]] deserved to be re-elected as well.<ref name=merc-ed>{{cite news
| newspaper=Mercury
| title=Our vote for stability
| date=1 July 2016
| page=46
}}</ref> The ''Advertiser'' welcomed Turnbull's decision to build 12 new submarines in South Australia.<ref name=tiser-ed /> The ''West Australian'' reminded voters to separate their judgement of Turnbull from their judgment of the less popular [[Barnett Ministry|Liberal state government]].<ref name=west-ed />

