==Campaign events==
{{see also|Debates of the Australian federal election, 2016}}
* 9 May – The House of Representatives and Senate are dissolved and the government enters [[Caretaker government of Australia|caretaker mode]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Fact check: is the Government not yet in caretaker mode|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-05-12/fact-check-is-the-government-not-yet-in-caretaker-mode/7409042|accessdate=12 May 2016|work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|location=Australia|date=12 May 2016}}</ref>
* 11 May – The Labor Party announces it will disendorse its candidate for [[Division of Fremantle|Fremantle]], Chris Brown, after it is revealed that he did not disclose two convictions dating back to the 1980s.<ref name="fremantle">{{cite news|title=Election 2016: Labor Fremantle candidate Chris Brown to be disendorsed by party|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-05-11/labor-candidate-chris-brown-to-be-disendorsed-by-alp/7406100|accessdate=11 May 2016|work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|location=Australia|date=11 May 2016}}</ref>
* 13 May – The [[Australian federal election debates, 2016|first televised people's forum]] involving Turnbull and Shorten is held in the Sydney suburb of [[South Windsor, New South Wales|South Windsor]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.tvtonight.com.au/2016/05/airdate-the-peoples-forum-with-malcolm-turnbull-bill-shorten.html|title=Airdate: The People’s Forum with Malcolm Turnbull & Bill Shorten|date=11 May 2016|accessdate=11 May 2016|first=David|last=Knox|work=[[TV Tonight]]}}</ref>
* 17 May – 
** Immigration minister [[Peter Dutton]] makes controversial comments about refugees during an interview on Sky News, stating that "many... won't be numerate or literate in their own language let alone English", that they "would be taking Australian jobs" and that "they would languish in unemployment queues and on Medicare".<ref name="refugees">{{cite news|title=Election 2016: Malcolm Turnbull backs 'outstanding' Peter Dutton after refugee comments|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-05-18/dutton-warns-illiterate-refugees-will-take-aussie-jobs/7424198|accessdate=19 May 2016|work=ABC News|date=18 May 2016}}</ref>

[[File:172 Clarke Street, Northcote, Victoria (2016-06-27) 02.jpg|thumb|David Feeney's $2.3&nbsp;million house in Northcote not declared on his parliamentary register of interests]]

** Labor MP for [[Division of Batman|Batman]], [[David Feeney]], admits that he had not declared his ownership of a $2.3&nbsp;million house in [[Northcote, Victoria|Northcote]], and that the house was [[Negative gearing|negatively-geared]]—a scheme that the Labor Party had promised to wind back if elected.
* 19 May – 
** Labor candidate for [[Division of Moore|Moore]] David Leith steps down after social media posts from 2015 are revealed in which he called Australia's immigration detention centres "gulags".<ref name="leith">{{cite news|title=Labor candidate for WA seat of Moore withdraws after 'gulag' comment about detention centres|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-05-19/labor-candidate-for-moore-withdraws/7429242|accessdate=25 May 2016|date=19 May 2016}}</ref>
**The [[Australian Federal Police]] conducts raids on the Melbourne offices of Labor senator [[Stephen Conroy]] and the home of a Labor advisor, over the alleged leak of documents regarding the status of the [[National Broadband Network]].<ref name="afpraids">{{cite news|title=AFP raids Labor party offices, home in Melbourne over leaked NBN documents|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-05-19/afp-raids-labor-party-offices-in-melbourne/7430346|accessdate=19 May 2016|work=ABC News|date=19 May 2016}}</ref>
* 20 May – 
**Treasury and the Department of Finance release the Pre-election Economic and Fiscal Outlook (PEFO).<ref name="pefo">{{cite news|title=PEFO budget numbers unchanged, Treasury stands by forecasts|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-05-20/pefo-forecasts-unchanged-from-budget/7432388|accessdate=20 May 2016|work=ABC News|date=20 May 2016}}</ref>
**Liberal candidate for Fremantle, Sherry Sufi, withdraws from nomination following controversy over his past comments on same-sex marriage and indigenous constitutional recognition, as well as a video of him mocking WA Speaker [[Michael Sutherland]].<ref name="sufi">{{cite news|title=Election 2016: Sherry Sufi quits as Liberal candidate for Fremantle|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-05-20/liberal-candidate-for-fremantle-sherry-sufi-resigns/7432644|accessdate=25 May 2016|work=ABC News|date=20 May 2016}}</ref>
* 24 May – 
**Treasurer [[Scott Morrison (politician)|Scott Morrison]] and finance minister [[Mathias Cormann]] hold a press conference in which they announce that their costing of Labor's policies has revealed a $67&nbsp;billion "black hole". Under media questioning, Morrison replies that while they had made some assumptions to arrive at the figure, it is up to Labor to clarify its policies. The Opposition responds, calling the announcement a "ridiculous scare campaign" that was "riddled with a litany of errors".<ref name="blackhole">{{cite news|title=Election 2016: Scott Morrison stands by claim of $67b black hole in Labor costings|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-05-24/labor-budget-has-$67-billion-black-hole-scott-morrison-says/7441804|accessdate=25 May 2016|work=ABC News|date=25 May 2016}}</ref>
**Labor Senator [[Nova Peris]] announces she will not nominate to contest her Northern Territory Senate seat at the election.<ref name="peris">{{cite news|title=Federal election 2016: Labor senator Nova Peris quits politics|url=http://www.smh.com.au/federal-politics/federal-election-2016/election-2016-labor-senator-nova-peris-quits-politics-20160524-gp2bk6.html|accessdate=26 May 2016|work=The Sydney Morning Herald|date=24 May 2016}}</ref>
*25 May – 
** Liberal candidate for [[Division of Whitlam|Whitlam]], Dr Carolyn Currie, withdraws from nomination citing a lack of support from the party's branch and factional leaders.<ref name="currie">{{cite news|title=Liberal candidate for Whitlam, Carolyn Currie, resigns citing lack of support|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-05-25/liberal-candidate-caroline-currie-seat-of-whitlam-resigns/7444334|accessdate=25 May 2016|work=ABC News|date=25 May 2016}}</ref>
** A regional leaders' debate between Nationals leader and Deputy Prime Minister [[Barnaby Joyce]], Labor MP [[Joel Fitzgibbon]], and Greens leader [[Richard Di Natale]] is held in [[Goulburn, New South Wales]]. The debate is moderated by [[Chris Uhlmann]] and broadcast on the ABC.<ref name="regleaders">{{cite news|title=Election 2016: Live exports and milk prices on agenda in Regional Leaders' Debate|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-05-25/election-live-blog-may-25/7442778|accessdate=25 May 2016|work=ABC News|date=25 May 2016}}</ref> During the debate, Joyce makes comments apparently linking a ban on live cattle exports to Indonesia with an increase in asylum seeker boats under the Gillard government.<ref name="cattle">{{cite news|title=Election 2016: Turnbull in damage control after Barnaby Joyce links asylum seekers to live exports|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-05-26/barnaby-joyce-downplays-asylum-seeker-live-cattle-comments/7447076|accessdate=27 May 2016|work=ABC News|date=26 May 2016}}</ref>
* 29 May – A leaders debate between Malcolm Turnbull and Bill Shorten is held at the National Press Club in Canberra.
* 9 June – Candidate nominations close.
* 11 June – Liberal candidate for [[Division of Calwell|Calwell]], John Hsu, resigns and is disendorsed. The deadline for nominations having closed results in the Liberals not running a candidate in that seat.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.news.com.au/finance/small-business/lib-brothelowner-quits-party/news-story/0c13c1798b6f551083a39031a8840138|title=Lib brothel-owner quits party|last=|first=|date=11 June 2016|work=|publisher=News Limited|access-date=13 June 2016|via=}}</ref> However, Hsu's name continues to be on the ballot paper and he continues to run as an independent.<ref>[http://www.news.com.au/national/federal-election/brothel-scandal-election-calwell-candidate-runs-as-independent/news-story/2baee321283bac212e9c2ffab0d48fa6 Brothel scandal election Calwell candidate runs as independent]</ref>
* 14 June – Pre-poll voting opens.
* 17 June – A third leaders debate between Malcolm Turnbull and Bill Shorten.<ref>[http://www.theage.com.au/federal-politics/federal-election-2016-opinion/federal-election-2016-the-third-election-debate-was-the-best-of-the-bunch-20160617-gpm2wk.html Federal election 2016: The third election debate was the best of the bunch]</ref>
* 19 June – Official launch of the ALP campaign.<ref>[http://www.theaustralian.com.au/federal-election-2016/federal-election-campaign-day-43-labors-campaign-launch-turnbull-in-sydney/news-story/264f376b98984db6f279fd4121868f43 Federal election campaign: Day 43, Labor’s campaign launch, Turnbull in Sydney]</ref>
* 20 June – Labor candidate for [[Division of Farrer|Farrer]], Christian Kunde, resigns and is disendorsed.<ref name="kunde">{{cite news|title=Election 2016: Labor candidate Christian Kunde steps down over Hizb ut-Tahrir reports|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-06-20/labor-candidate-quits-after-islamic-extremism-links-criticism/7524718|accessdate=20 June 2016|work=ABC News|date=20 June 2016}}</ref>

== Campaign advertising ==

Labor, campaign advertising began in February, with the Government airing taxpayer-funded commercials for its Innovation and Science Agenda that echoed Turnbull's catchphrase "exciting time". The ads were criticised by the Opposition, which referred the matter to the Auditor-General for investigation.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-03-16/government's-'exciting-time'-catchcry-'should-be-investigated'/7249684|title=Labor calls for use of Government's 'exciting time' catchcry in ideas boom ad to be investigated|first=Dan|last=Conifer|date=16 March 2016}}</ref>

The first negative shots in the campaign's television advertising war were fired in April, as the Government worked to obtain its second trigger for a double dissolution.

* "If Malcolm Turnbull gets his way" was created by the [[Construction, Forestry, Mining and Energy Union|Construction, Forestry, Mining & Energy Union]]. The ad depicted a drug dealer and a construction worker in police interview rooms, and claimed that if the ABCC was re-established as the Coalition intended, "a worker will have less rights than an [[Methamphetamine|ice]] dealer." <ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-04-27/will-building-workers-have-less-rights-than-ice-dealers-abcc/7340868|date=27 April 2016|title=Will building workers have less rights than ice dealers?|author=Australian Broadcasting Corporation}}</ref>
* "Bob Hawke speaks out for Medicare" (Labor). This attack ad, released on 11 June, featured former Prime Minister [[Bob Hawke]] claiming that Turnbull would "privatise" Medicare. Hawke, making his first appearance in a campaign ad since [[Australian federal election, 1990|1990]], referred to a "Medicare privatisation taskforce" established by the Coalition. Hawke's claims were rejected by the Government, the Australian Medical Association and the Royal Australian College of General Practitioners. Turnbull called it the "biggest lie" of the campaign. Nonetheless, the ad was highly effective, forcing the Coalition to scrap a plan to outsource management of the Medicare electronic payments system.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.theaustralian.com.au/opinion/columnists/peter-van-onselen/labor-plays-fast-and-loose-with-bob-hawkes-medicare-legacy/news-story/f12a7e1330c61bcc4a2e34ae8f378a69|first=Peter|last=van Onselen|newspaper=The Australian|title=Labor plays loose with Medicare legacy|date=25 June 2016}}</ref><ref name=amahead>{{cite news|url=http://www.theaustralian.com.au/federal-election-2016/federal-election-2016-ama-head-calls-out-shortens-medicare-scare/news-story/d2cb0137d08e38ec02f5c66a144f65f5|newspaper=The Australian|date=22 June 2016|title=AMA head calls out Shorten's Medicare scare|first=David|last=Crowe}}</ref>
* "Labor's war on the economy" (Liberal). This attack ad, released on 19 June, featured a supposed tradesman warning of what he characterised as Labor's desire to "go to war" with banks, mining companies and people with investment properties. Labor, union officials and many on social media initially dismissed the tradesman as "Fake Tradie"—although media outlets confirmed the actor was in fact a tradesman.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.smh.com.au/federal-politics/federal-election-2016/federal-election-2016-fake-tradie-andrew-macrae-says-hes-the-real-deal-20160621-gpo7tz.html|newspaper=Sydney Morning Herald|first=Georgina|last=Mitchell|date=21 June 2016|title='Fake tradie' Andrew MacRae reveals how he got the gig}}</ref>
* "Not this time, Tony" (The Nationals). [[National Party of Australia|The Nationals]] faced a strong challenge in their leader's seat of [[Division of New England|New England]] from independent former MP [[Tony Windsor]]. The party released an attack ad on 25 June that the previously conservative Windsor had supported the minority Labor government from 2010–13. The ad used the metaphor of an unfaithful ex-boyfriend asking for a second chance—with the woman replying by text message 'Not this time, Tony'. Windsor claimed the ad accused him of sexual infidelity, a suggestion rejected by the Nationals.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2016/jun/27/tony-windsor-demands-nationals-apologise-to-wife-over-gutter-attack-ad|newspaper=The Guardian Australia|date=27 June 2016|first=Gabrielle|last=Chan|title=Tony Windsor demands Nationals apologise to wife over 'gutter' attack ad}}</ref>
* Member for [[Division of Kennedy|Kennedy]] [[Bob Katter]] posted a video to YouTube on 14&nbsp;June showing people labelled 'Labor' and 'Liberal' putting up a 'For Sale' sign on Australia before cutting to a shot of the two lying on the ground while Katter himself held a gun facing the camera. Although Katter claimed the video was humorous, it attracted controversy as it was aired a few days after the [[2016 Orlando nightclub shooting]]. The ad was created with the help of the writers of satirical newspaper website ''[[The Betoota Advocate]]''.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.smh.com.au/federal-politics/federal-election-2016/bob-katter-ad-barry-osullivan-calls-for-resignation-20160615-gpk3j8.html|title=Bob Katter ad: Barry O'Sullivan calls for resignation|first=Amy|last=Mitchell-Wittington|newspaper=Brisbane Times|date=15 June 2016}}</ref>

In addition to the major political parties, other organisations aired their own issue ads. These included the [[Australian Council of Trade Unions]], the [[Royal Australian College of General Practitioners]], Master Builders Australia and the [[Australia Institute]].<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The Australian|title=Federal election campaign, day 33|url=http://www.theaustralian.com.au/federal-election-2016/federal-election-campaign-2016-day-33/news-story/04cf9933f83858eae05bde97910500d6|date=10 June 2016}}</ref><ref name=amahead />

In Australian federal elections, a "blackout" of campaign ads on radio and television applies from midnight on the Wednesday before polling day. On the eve of the blackout in 2016, commercial television stations, media analysts and some politicians called for this rule to be reviewed in the light of declining audience share for traditional broadcasters, and the growing role of the internet in campaigning.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-06-29/election-2016-blackout-broadcasting-political-advertising/7551190|first=Nathan|last=Stitt|newspaper=ABC News|title= Blackout on broadcast ads 'pointless' in internet age|date=29 June 2016}}</ref>

